WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.235 --> 00:00:01.650
(bright music)

00:00:01.650 --> 00:00:04.410
- Driverless taxi company
Cruise has had a bumpy month.

00:00:04.410 --> 00:00:07.320
Its autonomous driving permits
were suspended in California

00:00:07.320 --> 00:00:10.260
after a woman was dragged 20
feet under one of its vehicles.

00:00:10.260 --> 00:00:12.810
The company then paused
operations in every single one

00:00:12.810 --> 00:00:16.350
of its US markets to focus
on rebuilding public trust.

00:00:16.350 --> 00:00:18.120
And that could be a challenge.

00:00:18.120 --> 00:00:19.470
See, as driverless companies try

00:00:19.470 --> 00:00:21.660
to increase public
acceptance of their tech,

00:00:21.660 --> 00:00:22.680
they're rolling out more

00:00:22.680 --> 00:00:24.570
and more vehicles onto public roads.

00:00:24.570 --> 00:00:26.370
But that increases the probability

00:00:26.370 --> 00:00:28.200
that one of these cars
could have an accident

00:00:28.200 --> 00:00:29.370
that damages the paint work

00:00:29.370 --> 00:00:31.200
and the reputation of the technology.

00:00:31.200 --> 00:00:33.540
While every safe journey
may be one step forward,

00:00:33.540 --> 00:00:36.000
every collision can be two steps back.

00:00:36.000 --> 00:00:38.820
A recent AAA study found that
the number of Americans afraid

00:00:38.820 --> 00:00:41.640
of self-driving technology
has actually increased.

00:00:41.640 --> 00:00:44.370
So how can driverless car
companies rebuild trust

00:00:44.370 --> 00:00:45.930
after a crash?

00:00:45.930 --> 00:00:47.280
Okay, so in case you missed it,

00:00:47.280 --> 00:00:50.610
here's how Cruise lost
its permits in California,

00:00:50.610 --> 00:00:53.310
On October 2nd at around
9:30 PM on the corner

00:00:53.310 --> 00:00:55.620
of 5th and Market Street in San Francisco,

00:00:55.620 --> 00:00:58.140
a pedestrian was hit by a
car, not the Cruise one,

00:00:58.140 --> 00:01:01.140
and fell into the path of a
driverless Cruise vehicle.

00:01:01.140 --> 00:01:03.510
The Cruise vehicle braked hard and stopped

00:01:03.510 --> 00:01:06.090
and then attempted to pull
over to the side of the road.

00:01:06.090 --> 00:01:07.320
The problem was that the pedestrian

00:01:07.320 --> 00:01:10.380
who was struck was actually
still under the Cruise vehicle,

00:01:10.380 --> 00:01:13.620
and according to the DMV,
was pulled 20 feet forwards

00:01:13.620 --> 00:01:15.780
of speeds of up to seven miles an hour.

00:01:15.780 --> 00:01:17.460
The DMV said this may have caused

00:01:17.460 --> 00:01:19.140
further injury to the pedestrian.

00:01:19.140 --> 00:01:22.560
Now, Cruise said it spoke to
the DMV about the incident,

00:01:22.560 --> 00:01:24.630
but the DMV said Cruise didn't mention

00:01:24.630 --> 00:01:26.880
that the vehicle had
attempted to pull over.

00:01:26.880 --> 00:01:28.950
Cruise, however, said it showed the DMV,

00:01:28.950 --> 00:01:30.990
a full video of the incident.

00:01:30.990 --> 00:01:34.350
California's DMV then suspended
Cruise's driverless license

00:01:34.350 --> 00:01:36.720
to carry paying passengers and crucially,

00:01:36.720 --> 00:01:39.060
it's license to test on public roads,

00:01:39.060 --> 00:01:40.560
saying the manufacturer's vehicles

00:01:40.560 --> 00:01:42.990
are not safe for the public's operation.

00:01:42.990 --> 00:01:44.430
So if Cruise isn't allowed

00:01:44.430 --> 00:01:46.320
to be conducting driverless operations

00:01:46.320 --> 00:01:49.590
on roads in California,
how can it rebuild trust?

00:01:49.590 --> 00:01:50.790
- It's particularly important

00:01:50.790 --> 00:01:52.260
that the company does figure out a way

00:01:52.260 --> 00:01:54.840
just because California
is its biggest market.

00:01:54.840 --> 00:01:57.450
- That's WSJ's autos reporter Ryan Felton.

00:01:57.450 --> 00:01:58.530
- You're not gonna ever feel

00:01:58.530 --> 00:02:00.870
that it's safe until you
actually experience it.

00:02:00.870 --> 00:02:04.050
It's hard to imagine what
other sort of scenarios exist

00:02:04.050 --> 00:02:07.320
to rebuild that trust other
than just seeing through

00:02:07.320 --> 00:02:09.300
that people experience it.

00:02:09.300 --> 00:02:11.520
- With its licenses revoked in California,

00:02:11.520 --> 00:02:13.380
Cruise may have to turn
to the other states

00:02:13.380 --> 00:02:16.170
it was operating in to get
back out there and demonstrate

00:02:16.170 --> 00:02:18.900
that its technology can
be safe in the real world.

00:02:18.900 --> 00:02:20.550
But there is still a path for the company

00:02:20.550 --> 00:02:23.130
to resume driverless
operations in California.

00:02:23.130 --> 00:02:25.800
- The DMV has sort of spelled
out to the company it says,

00:02:25.800 --> 00:02:28.740
steps it can take to get back on the road,

00:02:28.740 --> 00:02:29.640
get its permit.

00:02:29.640 --> 00:02:31.770
We don't know what those
steps are at this point.

00:02:31.770 --> 00:02:34.590
- But the fact that the DMV
is regulating this industry

00:02:34.590 --> 00:02:37.950
and revoking real licenses
from real robotaxis

00:02:37.950 --> 00:02:40.980
in the real world when they
have real safety issues

00:02:40.980 --> 00:02:42.900
is a sign that driverless technology

00:02:42.900 --> 00:02:45.840
is in some way finally real.

00:02:45.840 --> 00:02:48.750
- Like, it's happening
and barring some sort of

00:02:48.750 --> 00:02:51.090
full scale pullback across the US,

00:02:51.090 --> 00:02:53.880
which seems highly
unlikely this is happening.

00:02:53.880 --> 00:02:56.190
There's mechanisms in place to take action

00:02:56.190 --> 00:02:57.750
when something does happen,

00:02:57.750 --> 00:02:59.580
then I think that's what you're seeing.

00:02:59.580 --> 00:03:01.650
- Now, Cruise isn't the
only driverless car company

00:03:01.650 --> 00:03:02.880
operating in the US.

00:03:02.880 --> 00:03:05.040
And while it has been
experiencing setbacks,

00:03:05.040 --> 00:03:07.200
others have been making headway.

00:03:07.200 --> 00:03:08.130
Alphabet's Waymo, for example,

00:03:08.130 --> 00:03:11.520
recently expanded its
operations across San Francisco

00:03:11.520 --> 00:03:13.110
and announced that it was offering rides

00:03:13.110 --> 00:03:15.150
through Uber's platform in Phoenix.

00:03:15.150 --> 00:03:18.090
But setbacks for one
company can have impacts

00:03:18.090 --> 00:03:20.490
on the reputation of the
technology as a whole.

00:03:20.490 --> 00:03:21.540
And what's not clear is

00:03:21.540 --> 00:03:24.390
how much further driverless
technology will have to go

00:03:24.390 --> 00:03:26.970
to convince the majority of
the public that it's safe

00:03:26.970 --> 00:03:29.340
or how many accidents
will happen along the way.

00:03:29.340 --> 00:03:31.320
A ladies mayor recently
wrote to the president

00:03:31.320 --> 00:03:33.510
of California's Public
Utilities Commission

00:03:33.510 --> 00:03:36.210
to voice serious concerns about a decision

00:03:36.210 --> 00:03:39.210
to approve an autonomous
vehicle program in the city.

00:03:39.210 --> 00:03:40.740
And there are still those who believe

00:03:40.740 --> 00:03:42.480
that Cruise has rolled out its technology

00:03:42.480 --> 00:03:44.340
on public roads too quickly.

00:03:44.340 --> 00:03:46.170
Cruise, for its part, has since announced

00:03:46.170 --> 00:03:47.490
that they're taking a number of steps

00:03:47.490 --> 00:03:49.440
to improve how the company operates

00:03:49.440 --> 00:03:52.170
including issuing a
voluntary software recall.

00:03:52.170 --> 00:03:53.100
- The vast majority of things

00:03:53.100 --> 00:03:54.480
are a hundred percent foolproof.

00:03:54.480 --> 00:03:58.170
That is just the reality here
that we're at a point now

00:03:58.170 --> 00:03:59.850
where there are companies actually

00:03:59.850 --> 00:04:02.310
using this technology
actually commercializing it.

00:04:02.310 --> 00:04:03.270
There's going to be incidents

00:04:03.270 --> 00:04:05.400
that happen just like, if a driver,

00:04:05.400 --> 00:04:07.080
a human driver gets into a crash

00:04:07.080 --> 00:04:09.210
that there could be ramifications as well.

00:04:09.210 --> 00:04:11.400
- So while Cruise might not
be operating autonomously

00:04:11.400 --> 00:04:13.440
on Californian roads in the near future,

00:04:13.440 --> 00:04:17.580
the October 2nd incident is
unlikely to be the last incident

00:04:17.580 --> 00:04:20.280
that an autonomous vehicle
experiences on its road

00:04:20.280 --> 00:04:22.157
to building public trust.

00:04:22.157 --> 00:04:24.740
(gentle music)

